        "original_id": "fc4f5128-a5a1-5ecd-a25e-b7fa8603869e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Exochorda, lime\n- Families: Rosaceae, citrus\n- Places: China, Central Asia, Turkestan\n- Concepts: taxonomy, hybridization, habitat loss, geographic separation\n\nFacts:\n- Exochorda is a small genus of flowering plants in the family Rosaceae that is native to China and Central Asia, including Turkestan.\n- Exochorda has had numerous species described based on differences in appearance and geographic separation.\n- A systematic study of Exochorda found that the different types are closely related and probably descended from a single formerly widespread species whose range was fragmented by habitat loss.\n- A lime is a hybrid citrus fruit.\n- The word lime came into English through French from Arabic līma and ultimately from Persian līmū, meaning lemon.\n- A lime is typically round, lime green, about 3 inches in diameter, and contains acidic juice vesicles.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
